Fixed in `lib/plugin-override.js:76`.

**Change** — `buildRoutePrefix` now only prepends the separating `/` when the parent prefix doesn't already end with one:

```js
if (instancePrefix.endsWith('/')) {
  if (pluginPrefix[0] === '/') {
    pluginPrefix = pluginPrefix.slice(1)   // avoid '/first//second'
  }
} else if (pluginPrefix[0] !== '/') {
  pluginPrefix = '/' + pluginPrefix
}
```

The only behavior that changes is the previously-broken case: parent `/v1/` + child `inner` now yields `/v1/inner` instead of `/v1//inner`. The other three combinations (`/v1/`+`/inner`, `/v1`+`inner`, `/v1`+`/inner`) and the empty-parent cases are unchanged — I compared the old and new joining side by side to confirm.

**Test** — added `'Prefix with trailing / and nested prefix without leading /'` to `test/route-prefix.test.js`, next to the existing `'Prefix with trailing /'` test, asserting `GET /v1/inner/route` resolves. `node --test test/route-prefix.test.js` passes 27/27.

Two notes on verification:
- I did not re-run the new test against the unpatched source in the repo — doing so cleanly would have meant stashing or checking out over my edit, which this worktree's rules discourage. Instead I verified the logic difference directly (output above): the old function returns `/v1//inner` for that input, so the test would 404 before the change.
- Per your instruction I ran only this one test file; I did not run lint, the full suite, the type tests, or the coverage check that the issue text mentions.

`COMPLEX.md` lists no hotspot, load-bearing, co-change, or seam entry for `lib/plugin-override.js`, so no partner file was implicated. Nothing committed.

